 "posts": [
  {
   "title": "The last chairmaker in the valley",
   "slug": "the-last-chairmaker",
   "date": "2026-11-10",
   "cats": [
    "Issue 9"
   ],
   "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1788296817269-8df0458ab799?w=1600&q=80&fm=jpg&fit=max",
   "excerpt": "Forty years, one pattern, about nine thousand chairs. Owen Pryce on why he never changed it.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Owen Pryce has made the same chair since 1986: ash legs, an elm seat, eleven spindles. He reckons he has made about nine thousand of them, and he can tell you where a surprising number went.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We spent two days in his workshop above the river, where the lathe is older than he is and the radio has been on the same station since he moved in. He talked about green wood, about the apprentices who stayed and the ones who did not, and about why a chair should be slightly too upright.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>He is seventy-one. Nobody is waiting to take over. He is surprisingly cheerful about that.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "How to read a board",
   "slug": "how-to-read-a-board",
   "date": "2026-10-28",
   "cats": [
    "Issue 9"
   ],
   "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1659930087003-2d64e33181f7?w=1600&q=80&fm=jpg&fit=max",
   "excerpt": "Grain, figure, tension: what a plank is telling you before you cut it.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A board is a record of a tree’s life, written sideways. Before a single cut, a good maker has looked at the end grain, sighted down the edge and knocked on it like a door.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is a short field guide to doing the same: which way the grain runs out, where the board will move when it dries, and how to tell reaction wood before it pinches your saw.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "A week at the green-wood school",
   "slug": "green-wood-school",
   "date": "2026-10-14",
   "cats": [
    "Issue 9"
   ],
   "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1759523146335-0069847ceb16?w=1600&q=80&fm=jpg&fit=max",
   "excerpt": "Twelve strangers, one felled ash and a shed in Herefordshire.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>On Monday there was a tree. By Friday there were twelve stools, eleven spoons and one very good ladle. Our writer went for the week and came back with blisters and opinions.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Working wood green, straight from the log, is fast, quiet and forgiving. It is also the way almost everything was made until about two hundred years ago.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "Where the timber comes from",
   "slug": "where-the-timber-comes-from",
   "date": "2026-09-30",
   "cats": [
    "Issue 9"
   ],
   "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1634672652995-ee7525bce595?w=1600&q=80&fm=jpg&fit=max",
   "excerpt": "Following one oak from a Shropshire hedge to a kitchen table.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The oak came down in a storm in 2021. It spent a year in the round, two years in stick, and three weeks in a kiln. We followed it the whole way, and met everyone who touched it.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There are fewer small sawmills every year. The ones that are left are worth knowing about.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "In praise of the hand plane",
   "slug": "in-praise-of-the-hand-plane",
   "date": "2026-09-16",
   "cats": [
    "Issue 9"
   ],
   "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793628012204-e62036a0462f?w=1600&q=80&fm=jpg&fit=max",
   "excerpt": "It is slower than a machine. It is also the best ten minutes of the day.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A machine planer will flatten a board in seconds and leave it looking sanded. A hand plane takes ten minutes and leaves it looking like glass.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is a love letter, with some practical notes on sharpening at the end, because the first is no use without the second.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  },
  {
   "title": "The joint that holds everything",
   "slug": "the-joint-that-holds-everything",
   "date": "2026-09-02",
   "cats": [
    "Issue 9"
   ],
   "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1789654499278-0ad599c8c241?w=1600&q=80&fm=jpg&fit=max",
   "excerpt": "A short history of the dovetail, and why drawers still need it.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The Egyptians cut dovetails. So does the machine that made your kitchen drawers, though it would rather you did not look too closely.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A joint that resists being pulled apart in exactly the direction a drawer is pulled: four thousand years on, nobody has had a better idea.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->"
  }
 ],
